{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cfsv31Y3bsvNV5wN5rfRN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good — these are the kind of constraints that actually make the site better, not harder. Updating the plan with all of this folded in, and flagging the handful of calls I made so you can veto them.\n\n## What changes\n\n**The near-miss stays, but un-narrated.** No bed number, no initial, no drug, no \"Mr D.\" — ever. My judgment call: instead of telling it as a scene, it becomes a single abstracted line of motivation, close to your own words but stripped of every detail that could identify anyone or anyone's shift: *\"Eleven years in, one near-miss was enough — not because it was rare, but because it was preventable.\"* No night, no ward layout, no specifics. **Tell me if you'd rather it not appear at all** — I can build the whole site without it; the workarounds study alone carries the \"why\" just fine.\n\n**The whiteboard photo is dropped entirely**, no cropping or blurring workaround — too risky. The empty handover sheets (before and after) become the one physical artifact on the site, which is better anyway: they're clean, they're yours to show, and they're the actual thing a hiring manager in this field will recognise.\n\n**Title fixed everywhere**: CNM1, and Nursing Informatics Link Nurse, one day a week. \"Clinical Informatics Lead\" doesn't appear anywhere, including meta/SEO text. Hospital named as text only (St Brendan's University Hospital), no logo asset anywhere.\n\n**71%→94% told as a ward result, not a solo one.** Copy will read as a team effort — pharmacy, IT, night staff, you — with your actual part named precisely: forty hours of observation, finding the three workarounds, getting the scanners moved to the medication room door. The handover sheet redesign is credited as a group of five you led, not a solo project.\n\n**Structural idea I'd like to float**: you work from the audit cycle — Plan, Do, Study, Act — every day. Your workarounds study *is* a PDSA cycle (Plan/Do = the 40 hours and the three workarounds found; Study/Act = the fixes and the 71→94 result). I'd like to label that section explicitly as PDSA rather than inventing a different narrative frame — it's true to how you actually think, and it signals real audit literacy to clinical-informatics hiring managers specifically. **Flag it if you'd rather keep it unlabeled and just tell it as a story.**\n\n**ISBAR gets used structurally, not just mentioned.** The handover sheet section walks through the five letters — Identify, Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation — as five short scroll beats, each one tied to what you actually changed in the redesign. Every Irish nurse recognising the form on sight is the point; it doesn't need explaining to that audience, so I won't over-explain it, but a one-line gloss per letter helps non-nursing hiring managers at health-tech companies follow along.\n\n## Revised scroll, top to bottom\n\n1. **Open** — name, real title, hospital named, status line: *open to clinical informatics roles, hospitals and health-tech, from 2026*. Your quote about the safe thing and the slow thing.\n2. **Why** — the abstracted near-miss line (or cut, per your call), framed as a principle, not an anecdote.\n3. **Plan** — the observational study: 40 hours, 22 nurses, 29% missed scans, three workarounds (named, no patient detail attached to any of them — the wristbands/scanner-location/printed-sheet findings are process facts, not patient facts, so they're safe to show in full).\n4. **Do** — the fixes, credited to the ward team, your specific part highlighted. Blank handover sheet shown here too, ISBAR-structured, credited to your group of five.\n5. **Study/Act** — the result: 71%→94% in nine months, handover sheet now on 6 wards, 14 nurses precepted, falls/pressure-ulcer dashboards. Numbers count up on scroll because they're real and they're the ward's.\n6. **Now** — your actual daily toolkit: ISBAR handover, early warning score chart, care plan, audit cycle. Role (CNM1 + Nursing Informatics Link Nurse), MSc Health Informatics in progress, SQL (basic), Power BI.\n7. **Close** — the hiring CTA stated plainly, CV download, LinkedIn, contact. Closes on the opening line.\n\n## Colour, type, motion, mobile\n\nUnchanged from before — night-ward palette, serif/mono/sans pairing, one restrained 3D object, ECG spine, scroll count-ups on real numbers, mobile degrading to swipe/CSS instead of scroll-jacked WebGL, `prefers-reduced-motion` respected throughout.\n\n## Still need from you\n\n1. A yes/no on the abstracted near-miss line, or confirmation to cut it.\n2. A yes/no on labeling the fix section as PDSA explicitly.\n3. The empty before/after handover sheets (image or scan).\n4. Confirmation \"St Brendan's University Hospital\" is the name you want used publicly (vs. a placeholder you've been using in these notes).\n5. A CV/resume file to link for the hiring CTA, and whether you want a contact form or a plain mailto to your email.\n\nOnce those are settled, say go and I'll start building."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6307,"cache_read_input_tokens":18522,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6307},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6e0b3569-c647-43f6-aa77-eb6fa69d958b","uuid":"7f81b061-99e1-4310-b206-d1a5a9e42d98","timestamp":"2026-10-10T04:21:45.519Z","request_id":"req_011Cfsv31Le25KdZ9FLVCE4W"}
